Travel Advisory Level 2: Exercise Increased Caution Travel Advisory Level 2: Exercise increased cautionin the United Kingdom due to terrorism. Read More...
Edward Heartney
Minister Counselor to ECOSOC
New York, New York
March 25, 2025
AS DELIVERED
The United States appreciates the work of France and Brazil in facilitating the process on this important resolution. We deeply regret that even after multiple members broke silence, there were no textual accommodations in pursuit of consensus.
The United States does appreciate the efforts by several members to put forward strong language on the importance of healthy food and nutrition. The United States strongly supports efforts to ensure children have the opportunity to receive healthy and nutritious meals and recognizes that school is a critical source of these meals for many. Healthy meals are an essential platform to prevent childhood chronic disease, ensure adequate development, and improve engagement of children and youth in schools.
We also support the language that emphasizes the importance of everyone having access to food that that is healthy, abundant and affordable, to better allow for full physical and mental development for all people and the prevention of chronic diseases. We support efforts to reverse the rising trends in overweight and obesity and reduce the burden of diet-related non-communicable diseases in all age groups.
In the United States we are not immune to these challenges and are turning the page to Make America Healthy Again. In our efforts, nutrition will play a major role in promoting health rather than just managing disease.
However, the extension of another ‘international decade’ or ‘theme day’ that fails to meet its goals signals that this approach is not an effective catalyst of change. Additionally, the text of this resolution contains several positions we cannot support, which compels us to call the vote today.
The United States has made clear any reaffirmation of the 2030 Agenda or the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) advances a program of soft global governance that is inconsistent with U.S. sovereignty and adverse to the rights and interests of Americans. As a matter of course the United States will not accept such reaffirmations.
This resolution contains terms and concepts that promote immoral discrimination and advance harmful gender ideology in defiance of biological reality, both of which conflict with U.S. policies. President Trump set a clear and overdue course correction on “gender ideology” and we must acknowledge the biological realities of sex to support the needs and perspectives of women and girls.
The United States provides the following further positions on specific language in the resolution:
Concerning language that we use to describe development, the United States believes it is time to start talking about “responsible” and “long-term” development that aims to move countries up and out of “developing” status. The term “sustainable” fails to indicate the direction and movement we all expect to see.
Concerning human rights, it is the long-standing U.S. position that all human beings have the same internationally recognized human rights and there are no gradations of human rights. Accordingly, it is redundant and unnecessary to list specific categories of people when speaking of human rights.
While the United States will not ignore threats to our natural environment, President Trump has been clear that the government of the United States must first and foremost refocus on the interests of Americans.
The preambular climate references in this resolution are an unnecessary distraction from addressing critical health and nutrition issues. The United States does not support these climate change references in this resolution.
On technology transfer, the United States has long been clear that such agreements must be on both voluntary and mutually agreed terms.
The United States further brings to attention its intention to withdraw from the World Health Organization and does not support language “appreciating” the Organization.
Lastly, the United States underscores that UN General Assembly resolutions are non-binding documents that do not create rights or obligations under international law, including regarding rights related to food. The United States is not a party to the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights and interprets this resolution’s references to the right to food with respect to States Parties to that covenant, in light of its Article 2(1).
Thank you.
