Jonathan Shrier
Acting ECOSOC Representative
New York, New York
March 24, 2025
AS DELIVERED
Thank you, co-facilitators. The United States is deeply concerned about the current draft and reiterates the importance of a concise, voluntary, and non-binding FfD4 outcome document. The document remains far too long and too prescriptive.
Above all, the United States rejects and denounces the ever-widening definition of sustainable development and efforts to redefine authorities and mandates of the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development and the SDGs, and we will no longer reaffirm either as a matter of course.
The international financial institutions have independent mandates and authorities, and we do not support attempts in the UN system to dictate their priorities or activities.
The first draft also places far too much focus on donors and insufficient focus on national responsibility for economic growth and development, a foundational element of the Monterrey Consensus in 2002 and all subsequent FfD conferences. The central role of domestic resources and national enabling environments for economic growth is not emphasized enough in the first revision.
We emphasize the centrality of the mobilization and effective use of domestic resources and strong partnerships with the private sector to encourage private investment, and the United States rejects the idea that national success is contingent upon increased donor funding.
Some recommendations encroach upon the sovereignty of states and the independence of other organizations, including the WTO, OECD, and international financial institutions. The document should refrain from calling on independent institutions or seeking to influence decisions or processes that are the purview of their governing bodies.
Certain proposals regarding tax, debt, and trade are redundant and would be better addressed in the relevant competent fora. Further, the UN has no authority to dictate trade policy or intellectual property law.
Additionally, the draft continues to propose the establishment of new, resource-intensive meetings and mechanisms, which is misaligned with our objective of prioritizing efficiency and consolidation, and at odds with the UN Secretary-General’s newly announced UN80 initiative.
In particular, the United States reiterates that we cannot accept text that calls for a UN intergovernmental process on debt or proposes a role for the UN in the global debt architecture for which it is neither mandated nor equipped. We also stress that this process should not address issues such as financial regulation or seek to intervene in the role of regulators.
Finally, the United States understands that this instrument does not create or otherwise change any rights or obligations under international law and reiterates that the text should make this position clear.
As a general principle, the United States is reaffirming the foundational principle of sovereignty in foreign affairs. Our primary responsibility is to our citizens — to meet their needs, ensure their safety, preserve their rights, and defend our values — and our approach to international development is shaped by this commitment.
Thank you.
