AS DELIVERED
Thank you, Mr. President.
The action in the Security Council on February 24 demonstrated that the United States is committed to ending the Russia-Ukraine war.
Hundreds of thousands of Ukrainians and Russians have died. The longer the war continues, the greater the suffering for both nations. The war must end now, and the peace must be durable and lasting, not a temporary pause.
With the adoption of Resolution 2774, the Security Council showed that the United Nations can still live up to the purposes and principles of its Charter – mainly, to maintain international peace and security, including through the peaceful settlement of disputes. We reminded the world that there is a common ground that we can all agree on: all UN Member States can agree that the war is a tragedy, and that it must end.
Security Council Resolution 2774 does not end the war, but it has put us on a path to peace. The United States is fully committed to playing a meaningful and constructive role in the pursuit of peace.
Mr. President, the Security Council has proven that the UN is committed to, and capable of helping to accomplish, its original purpose. We look forward to continued work in this regard.
I thank you.
