Remarks at the UN meeting entitled 58th Plenary Meeting of the General Assembly, Consideration of Draft Resolution (A/79/L.57/Rev.1) – United Nations Regional Centre for Sustainable Development Goals for Central Asia and Afghanistan
AS DELIVERED
The United States recognizes Kazakhstan’s efforts to reach consensus on this resolution. However, despite joining consensus, the United States remains skeptical of the need for this SDG Center and, in principle, opposed to expansion of the UN system, especially where the likelihood of duplication of efforts and overlapping mandates is high. Kazakhstan needs neither an expanded UN system nor the SDGs in order to prosper; it should, instead, make sovereign decisions for its people and cast aside the burden of soft global governance.
We are equally concerned when expansion occurs without prior preparation and consideration by Member States of a feasibility study and draft terms of reference. As UN General Assembly members, we have a duty to require due diligence and observe due process in considering the creation of any new UN-branded entities. We have a duty to ensure all UN entities, new and old, set and meet high standards for transparency, accountability, integrity, efficiency, and effectiveness.
In addition, the United States would like to explain our position on certain points addressed in this resolution:
It is time to end the inclusion and constant repetition of references to prior resolutions, and to look instead to the future. The inefficient practice of citing past agreements, including ones where many Member States issued strongly worded explanation of positions, with varying degrees of enthusiasm unnecessarily lengthens every resolution; fuels time-wasting debates about “noting,” “welcoming,” or doing something else; and diverts funds from program implementation to document translation. This change would deliver time and cost savings across the UN system and allow all delegations to focus greater attention on meaningful negotiation.
In addition, UN documents should avoid mentioning non-UN organizations, by name or category, in ways that either imply those entities have a decision-making role in or responsibilities to the UN system or that imply UN endorsement of those entities and their activities. While they may have priorities that enable cooperation with UN entities, non-UN organizations remain independent and self-governing.
Concerning language that we use to describe development, the United States believes it is time to start talking about “responsible” and “long-term” development that aims to move countries up and out of “developing” status. The term “sustainable” fails to indicate the direction and movement we all expect to see.
Concerning human rights, it is the long-standing U.S. position that all human beings have the same internationally recognized human rights and there are no gradations of human rights. Accordingly, it is redundant and unnecessary to list specific categories of people when speaking of human rights.
Thank you.
